  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for patchy frost Monday, Tuesday, and\nWednesday nights across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front currently draped along the St. Lawrence will\ncontinue to drop south today. 1029mb sfc high located over\nOntario will build south into the area tonight with skies\nclearing over the north. As high builds in may see deeper\nvalleys decouple acrs the northwest resulting almost ideal\nradn/l cooling conditions. Only caveat will be some semblance of\na pressure gradient may still exist leading to ocnl spurts of\nwind. The growing season has climatologically ended for areas\nnorth of Bangor as of this morning (Monday morning), though\nthose who still have tender vegetation should take steps to\nprotect them tonight. \n\nHigh pressure will be ridged across the region Tuesday and\nWednesday nights with clear skies and light winds. This will \nprovide favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Patchy/areas\nof frost are expected Tuesday and Wednesday nights across northern\nand central portions of the region. Temperatures could fall below\nfreezing across the normally colder northern low lying and valley\nlocations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low could impact at least Downeast\nMaine late this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA coastal low will develop offshore from the Mid-Atlantic region\nmid week. At the same time, high pressure will remain centered \nnorthwest of the region. The track of the ocean low through late\nweek is still uncertain. With blocking high pressure to the \nnorthwest, uncertainty still exists regarding the northern edge \nof the precipitation shield and whether rain will reach the \nforecast area.",
